<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
                      http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd"
    objectQuotingStrategy="QUOTE_ONLY_RESERVED_WORDS">

    <!-- How long the index build may wait for a lock; a Liquibase parameter of the same name overrides it. -->
    <property name="outboxIndexLockTimeout" value="30s"/>
    <!-- Set, change or remove that override only for the start that runs the changeset: Liquibase expands the
         parameter into the SQL before it computes the checksum, so once the changeset ran, an override set,
         changed or removed makes every later start fail validation (release-boundary.md, Release). -->

    <!--
      The outbox rows the ETL has not finished with (pending, failed, dead), in claim order. The ETL's due check,
      its claim and its backlog gauges read only these rows; without the index each of them reads the whole outbox,
      which keeps every row it was ever sent.

      A plain CREATE INDEX in the changeset's transaction. Liquibase runs at startup, before the application serves
      and with the previous one stopped, so nothing writes to the outbox meanwhile. A plain build takes a SHARE lock,
      which blocks writers only, and does not wait for older snapshots the way a concurrent build does (a pg_dump, a
      sync or an idle session could stall startup indefinitely). On production's 64,645 rows it takes tens of
      milliseconds. lock_timeout makes the deploy fail visibly, instead of waiting, if something holds a write lock.

      It runs while no valid index of that name exists, and marks itself ran otherwise. An invalid leftover (an
      interrupted concurrent build) is dropped and the index built normally.

      It replaces 20261124-01 (a concurrent build) and 20261124-02 (the same build, but accepting an invalid index
      as done), neither of which reached production; where either ran, the valid index exists and this changeset
      only marks itself ran.

      Rollback drops the index; nothing depends on it but query speed.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20261124-03-outbox-unfinished-index" author="datarun">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <sqlCheck expectedResult="0">
                SELECT count(*)
                FROM pg_index i
                JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = i.indexrelid
                WHERE c.relname = 'idx_outbox_unfinished' AND i.indisvalid
            </sqlCheck>
        </preConditions>
        <sql>SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '${outboxIndexLockTimeout}'</sql>
        <sql>DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_outbox_unfinished</sql>
        <sql>
            CREATE INDEX idx_outbox_unfinished
                ON outbox (submission_serial_number, outbox_id)
                WHERE status IN ('pending', 'failed', 'dead')
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '${outboxIndexLockTimeout}'</sql>
            <sql>DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_outbox_unfinished</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
